<h3>Privacy Policy</h3><h2><strong>I. Data Protection</strong></h2><h2><strong>A. Introduction</strong></h2><div>The protection of your personal data is important to us. Therefore, we handle your personal data in accordance with the provisions of data protection law (the Swiss Data Protection Act and in accordance with the EU GDPR) as well as the rules governing the legal profession. Below, we provide you with non-exhaustive information on how we collect and otherwise process personal data.</div><div><br><strong>B. Definition of Terms</strong></div><div>Personal data refers to all information relating to a natural person (e.g., names, location data, etc.).</div><div>“Processing” refers to any operation or set of operations performed on personal data, whether or not by automated means, such as collection, recording, organization, structuring, storage, adaptation or alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure by transmission, distribution, or any other form of disclosure, as well as the alignment or combination, restriction, erasure, or destruction of such data.</div><h2><strong>II. Data Controllers</strong></h2><div>The data controllers within the meaning of the General Data Protection Regulation are</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>LuksundVogt Rechtsanwältinnen</div><div>Stampfenbachplatz 4</div><div>8006 Zürich</div><div>Tel.: 044 363 13 13</div><div>Email: mail@luksundvogt.ch</div><div>Website: https://www.luksundvogt.ch</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>If you have any questions or concerns, please feel free to contact us using the information above.</div><h2><strong>III. General Information on Data Processing</strong></h2><div><strong>A. Scope of Personal Data Processing</strong></div><div>We process personal data primarily in the course of our legal services, specifically within the framework of our client relationships with clients based both in Switzerland and abroad. We also process personal data that we receive from clients or other individuals involved in the client relationship, as well as through the operation of our website.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>Your personal data will be disclosed to third parties<br>– if you expressly consent;<br>– if there is a legal obligation or authorization;<br>– if it is necessary to assert, exercise, or defend legal claims;<br>– to fulfill the client-attorney relationship.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>In the course of managing the client-attorney relationship, the data will be disclosed—to the extent permitted—in particular to the following entities (recipients):<br>– Clients and counterparties in Switzerland and abroad<br>– Domestic and foreign authorities, government agencies, or courts<br>– Other parties in potential or actual legal proceedings<br>– Auxiliary persons in Switzerland and abroad (e.g., attorneys, tax professionals, private investigators, accountants)</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><strong>B. Retention Period</strong></div><div>The data subject’s personal data will be retained for as long as necessary to fulfill contractual and legal obligations (e.g., retention requirements). The data may be retained for a longer period to assess any conflicts of interest or other legitimate business interests.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><strong>C. Use of the Website</strong></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><strong>1. Cookies</strong></div><div>We use cookies and similar technologies on our website to continuously improve our services. A cookie is a text file containing short data strings that cannot perform any operations on their own. This text file is transmitted from the web server to your browser when you visit a website. The cookie is then stored on your local computer. There are different types of cookies (session cookies and persistent cookies). Please note that certain cookies are set as soon as you visit our website.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>You can use your browser settings to determine whether and how cookies are allowed. Restricting cookies may result in limitations when visiting the website. If you do not consent to the use of cookies, please discontinue use of the website immediately.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><strong>2. Google Analytics</strong></div><div>We use the web analytics service Google Analytics to optimize our website and meet current needs. This is a web analytics service provided by Google Inc., 1600 Amphitheatre Parkway, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA. This service uses cookies that analyze the use of our website to gather information for the purpose of improving our services.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><strong>D. Security</strong></div><div>Your personal data will be treated confidentially and protected against loss, manipulation, and unauthorized access by third parties through appropriate technical and organizational measures (e.g., firewalls or relevant contracts).</div><h2><strong>IV. Rights of the Data Subject</strong></h2><div><strong>A. Options<br></strong>If your personal data is being processed, you are a data subject and have various options available to you with respect to the data controllers. You have the right to request access to your data, as well as to request rectification, restriction, data portability, or erasure. In addition, you may withdraw your consent to further data processing at any time with future effect.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><strong>B. Restrictions</strong></div><div>We may invoke legal restrictions (in particular, in the case of a retention obligation, the existence of an overriding interest, or the assertion of claims).</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div><strong>C. Exercising Your Rights</strong></div><div>If you have any questions, suggestions, or concerns regarding your data, please feel free to contact us by mail, phone, or email.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>If you contact us via email, please note that emails are not encrypted and are therefore subject to the security risks typical of this means of communication.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>The exercise of one or more of these rights must be submitted in writing to the address provided above, along with proof of identity. The exercise of these rights may conflict with any contractual agreements.<br>Any person whose data is processed in this context has the option to enforce their claims in court.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>A complaint may also be filed with the competent data protection authority. In Switzerland, the Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner is the competent data protection authority.</div><h2><strong>V. Obligations of the Data Subject</strong></h2><div>To ensure the proper conduct of the business relationship, it is necessary for you to provide us with the relevant personal data. Otherwise, it may not be possible to enter into or fulfill a contract, or such action may only be possible under more onerous conditions.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>When you provide us with personal data regarding other individuals, you must ensure that those individuals are aware of this Privacy Policy. You may only provide us with permissible and accurate personal data.</div><h2><strong>VI. Changes to the Privacy Policy</strong></h2><div>Changes to this Privacy Policy may become necessary at any time. We therefore reserve the right to amend the Privacy Policy at any time. The version currently published on our website shall apply. In the event of an update, we will notify you in an appropriate manner to the extent that the Privacy Policy governs the contractual relationship between you and us.</div>
